 ## Approach
 
-Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The approach for delivering committed changes is still open: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service. All three can meet the required freshness target. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. Committed changes reach browsers over WebSocket connections the project runs itself, with no realtime provider in the delivery path. The server publishes each committed event revision to the browser sessions subscribed to that event, so updates arrive in about as long as the network takes rather than on a fixed interval.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+Owning the connections means owning their failure modes. Change storage, delivery, and browser recovery are designed together around replay rather than around a live stream being reliable: the change store keeps event revisions in a replayable order and makes publication of a committed revision dependable, delivery manages socket sessions and per-event fan-out within a connection capacity we provision, and browsers track the last revision they hold so a reconnect is followed by a catch-up read rather than a blind resume. A browser that cannot reconnect shows a stale-connection state instead of a board that merely looks quiet.
 
